\title{#{\equiv_m} is an equivalence relation}
\date{2026-04-10T16:22:17Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\taxon{Theorem}

\p{#{\equiv_m} is an [equivalence relation](008Y) for any #{m \in \Z^+}, that is, it is [reflexive](008I), [symmetric](008J), and [transitive](008P).}

\proof{
  In fact, any relation which can be [characterized by canonical representatives](00E4) is automatically an equivalence relation, since it inherits the relevant properties from the equality relation.  For suppose #{a \sim b} iff #{f(a) = f(b)}.
  \ul{
    \li{Since equality is reflexive, #{f(a) = f(a)} for any #{a \in A} and thus #{a \sim a}.}
    \li{If #{a \sim b}, then #{f(a) = f(b)}; since equality is symmetric this means #{f(b) = f(a)} and thus #{b \sim a}.}
    \li{If #{a \sim b} and #{b \sim c}, then #{f(a) = f(b)} and #{f(b) = f(c)}; by transitivity of equality, this means #{f(a) = f(c)}, and thus #{a \sim c}.}
  }
}
